-- title:
--   Injectivity of 𝔽ₚ⊗ψᵥ for the Raynaud quotient
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero level $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit whose image under the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is trivial, and $M/p$ nonzero. Fix a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for which $p$ is a nonunit (the predicate [`ValuationSubring.LiesOverPrime`](def/FLTPrelim_Ramification.html#L16)), with residue field of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed. Fix a witness `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤` generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the integral form ratios of full level, which is what allows the two-chart integral models `toBase p Γ hj` over $R_p =$ [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) to be formed.
--
--   The geometric input consists of: a Deligne–Rapoport-style integral model datum $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81) for the curve of level $\Gamma_M(H)$ over $R_p$, a level datum $\Lambda$ (a section $\sigma_{Pl}$ of the base over $Pl$ lifting the generic point, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base with a relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and identifications of its generic and special sections with the relevant Jacobian and Picard groups), and a Néron-object datum $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53) for $J_H = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H`: a smooth separated group object $O.G \to$ `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, an identification $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of $J_H(M,H)$ with its generic sections, Hecke endomorphisms, a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ whose section extends across the place $\sigma_{Pl}$) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$ (generated by the image of the toric points).
--
--   Two representability hypotheses are assumed, each as a nonempty type of `RepresentsRelSubPic` data: `hrep` states that the designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$ with the unit section as zero section represents, for the model `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor cut out by fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero (`algEquivZeroCut`); `hrepΛ` states the same for the model `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj`, rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$, and the designation built from $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$.
--
--   The coefficient ring is a type $R_h$ which is a commutative domain, henselian local and a discrete valuation ring, equipped with an algebra structure on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which is faithful, such that `hRA`: the image of $R_h$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lies in $Pl$, and `hRloc`: an element of $R_h$ lies in the maximal ideal exactly when its image has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$. Moreover $\mathbb{Z}/p$ is an $R_h$-algebra for which `hres` holds: the structural map $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$ kills exactly those elements whose image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has $Pl$-valuation $< 1$.
--
--   Over $R_h$ a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ of height $h$ is given (levels $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, finite free of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective transition bialgebra maps whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals), together with a comparison with $J_H$ consisting of: an additive map $\Delta$ from $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M,H)$ and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate modules $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,J_H(M,H)$, subject to the following hypotheses. `hΔinj`: $\Delta$ is injective. `hΔlev`: for every $v$, a class $y$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ if and only if $y = \Delta$ of (the image in $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}$ of) some level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. `hΔgal`: whenever $\tau'$ is an $R_h$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing pointwise with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\tau$, one has $\Delta(\tau' \cdot z) = \tau \cdot \Delta z$. `hΔhecke`: for every set $S$ of primes and every Hecke generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (a $T_\ell$, a $U_q$ or a diamond operator) there is a family $\varphi_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal{G}$, compatible with the transition maps, such that precomposition with $\varphi_v$ on level-$v$ points corresponds under $\Delta$ to the operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H$. `he`: the $n$-th component of $e\,x$ equals $\Delta$ applied to the $n$-th component of $x$. `heinj`: $e$ is injective. `herange`: $y$ lies in the range of $e$ if and only if each component $y_n$ lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$. `hegal`: $e$ intertwines the Tate-module representation $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{tateModuleRep}$ (for automorphisms $\tau'$ over $R_h$) with [`ModularCurve.JH.tateGaloisRep`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L154) (for the underlying $\tau$ over $\mathbb{Q}$). `hsat`: the range of $e$ is saturated, that is $p \cdot y$ in the range implies $y$ in the range. `hcoker`: the quotient of $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,J_H$ by the range of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{\,O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$. `htor`: every element of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ is $\Delta$ of a level-$v$ point.
--
--   A second $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R_h$ of height $hB$ is given, together with $R_h$-bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ and a natural number $h'$, subject to: `hhB`: $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$; `hhB2`: $hB = 2h'$; `hψt`: the $\psi_v$ commute with the transition maps; `hψker`: for a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the point of $\mathcal{B}$ obtained by composing $x$ with $\psi_v$ is trivial if and only if $\Delta(x) \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$; `hψsurj`: every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{B}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ arises in this way from a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$; `hψred`: if the $\psi_v$-image of $x$ reduces to the identity, in the sense that $Pl$-valuation of $x(\psi_v a) - \varepsilon(a)$ is $< 1$ for all $a \in \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ (where $\varepsilon$ is the counit, pushed into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ through $R_h$), then $x$ itself reduces to the identity in the same sense on $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$; `hperiod`: for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` and every level-$v$ point $y$ of $\mathcal{G}$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity in the above sense.
--
--   Finally the comparison is pinned at the level of schemes by a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$, subject to: `hρh`: $\rho_h$ followed by $R_h \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is the structural map $R_p \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hιbase`: $\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $R_h \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$; `hιcl`: the induced morphism to the pullback of $O.g$ along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$ is a closed immersion; `hιp`: $\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ for $O.L$ factors through the unit section, i.e. lands in the $p^v$-torsion; `hιpts`: the section $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ attached to a level-$v$ point $x$ is $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the algebra map of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$; `hιmul`: for every $R_h$-algebra $B$ and level-$v$ points $x, y$ over $B$ whose associated morphisms lie over the base (hypotheses `hx`, `hy`), the morphism attached to $x \cdot y$ is the $O.L$-product of the two; `hιt`: $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the transition map followed by $\iota_{v+1}$ equals $\iota_v$; `hιhecke`: for every $S$ and every Hecke generator $g$ there is a family $\varphi_v$ of $R_h$-bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels, compatible with the transitions, with $\mathrm{Spec}\,\varphi_v$ followed by $\iota_v$ equal to $\iota_v$ followed by $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$, and inducing `genOpH M H S g` through $\Delta$ on level-$v$ points; `hιfin`: for every $v$, the canonical morphism $j_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ to the base change along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho_h$ of the kernel of multiplication by $p^v$ (formed as the iterated pullback, with the two compatibility proofs $h3$, $h4$ as stated) is simultaneously an open immersion and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R_h$ belongs to the image of $j_v$.
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses, the conclusion is that for every $v \in \mathbb{N}$ the map
--   $$\mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \longrightarrow \mathbb{Z}/p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v,$$
--   namely the bialgebra homomorphism over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ obtained as the tensor product of the identity of $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with $\psi_v$, is injective.
--
--   This is the statement that the Raynaud-type quotient map $\psi$ of the $p$-divisible group attached to the finite part of the Néron object of $J_H$ at $p$ remains injective after reduction along $R_h \to \mathbb{Z}/p$, i.e. on the special fibre; it is the point at which the theory of $p$-divisible groups over a henselian discrete valuation ring is invoked. It is one conjunct of the larger package of data produced for the two-step tower and the idempotent/descent constructions attached to the finite part of $J_H$ at $p$, and is used by those statements.
